+{
+  "title": "mappingAS: Spatial Range Metrics, MapBiomas Habitat Conversion and Fire, and Protected-Area Overlap for IUCN Criterion B Screening",
+  "upload_type": "software",
+  "access_right": "open",
+  "license": "MIT",
+  "version": "1.8.0",
+  "language": "eng",
+  "creators": [
+    { "name": "Barreira, Antonio Lucas" }
+  ],
+  "keywords": [
+    "IUCN Red List",
+    "Criterion B",
+    "Extent of Occurrence",
+    "Area of Occupancy",
+    "extinction risk",
+    "preliminary conservation assessment",
+    "MapBiomas",
+    "land use and land cover",
+    "habitat conversion",
+    "fire",
+    "protected areas",
+    "Conservation Units",
+    "Brazil",
+    "R",
+    "Shiny",
+    "spatial ecology"
+  ],
+  "related_identifiers": [
+    {
+      "identifier": "https://github.com/lucasbarreirageo/mappingAS",
+      "relation": "isSupplementTo",
+      "scheme": "url"
+    }
+  ],
+  "description": "<p><strong>mappingAS</strong> (<em>Mapping Area of Species</em>) is an R package, with an accompanying Shiny application, for preliminary species extinction-risk screening under Criterion B of the IUCN Red List. Starting from a set of occurrence points, it estimates each species' geographic range and quantifies the pressures acting on it using Brazil's national datasets, in a workflow familiar to users of GeoCAT.</p><p>For every species it computes:</p><ul><li><strong>Range metrics</strong> - the Extent of Occurrence (EOO, minimum convex polygon) and the Area of Occupancy (AOO, 2 km grid) on a data-centred equal-area projection, with provisional Criterion B size categories (screening only).</li><li><strong>Habitat conversion</strong> - the percentage of converted (anthropic) versus remaining natural land cover within the EOO and AOO from MapBiomas, with a full per-class breakdown and land-cover time series (1985-2024).</li><li><strong>Fire</strong> - the share of the range burned at least once and burned-area time series, from MapBiomas Fire.</li><li><strong>Protected-area overlap</strong> - the overlap of occurrences, EOO and AOO with Brazilian federal Conservation Units (ICMBio / INDE), including the habitat that is both natural and protected.</li></ul><p>MapBiomas data are read locally over the network via GDAL '/vsicurl/' (no Google Earth Engine account required) or server-side through Google Earth Engine for large-scale assessments. Outputs include interactive Leaflet maps, publication-ready static maps, interactive charts, spatial (shapefile / GeoPackage) and raster (GeoTIFF) exports, and a written, referenced assessment report (HTML, plain text or Word). The bundled Shiny application ties the whole workflow together, with no code required.</p><p><strong>Note.</strong> The categories produced are provisional and intended for screening only; they do not replace a formal IUCN Red List assessment, which also requires the sub-conditions of fragmentation or few locations, continuing decline and extreme fluctuation.</p><p>Source code and documentation: <a href=\"https://github.com/lucasbarreirageo/mappingAS\">https://github.com/lucasbarreirageo/mappingAS</a></p>"
+}
